1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,240
Hello, dear listeners! My name is Aizhan Kumarkyzy Kairbayeva. I am a computer graphics instructor.

2
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:13,630
Welcome to the Blender software course!

3
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,090
The first video will be an introduction. Therefore, I will show you how to install the Blender software and introduce you to its interface.

4
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,000
I use Blender version 4.3.

5
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,000
You can download the program from the official website blender.org.

6
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:10,730
The website interface may change, but the Download Blender button is always in a prominent place.

7
00:01:10,730 --> 00:01:13,210
Clicking it will take you to the download page.

8
00:01:13,210 --> 00:01:16,790
Choose your Blender version, save the file, install, and launch the program.

9
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,690
When you first launch the program, you will be asked a few questions.

10
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:47,330
For example, if you have used Blender before, you can choose the settings from that version.

11
00:01:47,330 --> 00:01:52,630
It also suggests choosing which mouse button you will use to select objects: right or left.

12
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:54,830
— this does not affect the program's functionality.

13
00:01:54,830 --> 00:02:00,470
Additionally, the Spacebar key can be set to play animation (Play).

14
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,830
After launching Blender, you will always be greeted by the Splash Screen window, which you can exit by clicking outside of it.

15
00:02:15,690 --> 00:02:18,790
And now, after exiting the initial setup window,

16
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:22,150
you will enter the main Blender interface.

17
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:24,990
It is divided into several windows:

18
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,770
The largest is the 3D Viewport,

19
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:30,520
this is the main workspace,

20
00:02:31,370 --> 00:02:32,890
where objects are created and modified,

21
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
as well as scenes, materials, lighting, camera movement, and so on.

22
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:40,990
Below it

23
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,170
is the Timeline window — this is a timeline for placing keyframes

24
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:49,750
for object animation.

25
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:54,100
On the right is the Outliner window, where all objects in the scene are displayed in a schematic view.

26
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,340
They can be selected and grouped into collections.

27
00:02:57,570 --> 00:03:03,770
Collections are very convenient for working with large projects, where there are hundreds, or even thousands, of objects.

28
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:07,340
At the bottom are tabs with various settings.

29
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:12,000
We will look at them in the next videos, as they require separate attention.

30
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,660
And now I would like to briefly talk about the 3D-Viewport window in Blender.

31
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,130
Navigation in Blender is done using the mouse wheel.

32
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:06,500
If you press the wheel and rotate it, you can rotate the object around itself.

33
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,920
By default, rotation

34
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,390
occurs relative to an undefined point.

35
00:04:11,390 --> 00:04:14,400
To rotate relative to the selected object,

36
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:20,990
you need to go to Edit > Preferences > Navigation and enable the Orbit Around Selection parameter.

37
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:24,340
Be sure to enable this parameter — it significantly simplifies your work.

38
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,640
Now, after selecting an object, if you press the mouse wheel and rotate it, the view will move around that object.

39
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
For example, if you select the camera, you will orbit around the camera;

40
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,880
if you select a light source — you will orbit around the light source.

41
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,750
To zoom in or out, rotate the mouse wheel.

42
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
To move around the scene, press the Shift key and, while holding it, press the mouse wheel and move the mouse.

43
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,470
And now, let's review:

44
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,950
Pressing and moving the mouse wheel — rotating the object.

45
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:08,700
Rotating the mouse wheel — zooming in and out.

46
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,300
Shift and pressing with mouse wheel movement — moving left, right, up, down in the scene.

47
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,990
Hotkeys are used to move objects in the scene.

48
00:05:39,990 --> 00:05:43,000
For example, the G key allows you to move an object.

49
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,840
And to move only along the X, Y, or Z axes,

50
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,770
after pressing the G key, you need to press the X, Y, or Z key.

51
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
To rotate, you need to press the R key.

52
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,890
Rotation can only be done along the X, Y, or Z axes.

53
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
And to change the size, you need to press the S key.

54
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,970
The size can be changed along the X, Y, or Z axes.

55
00:06:04,650 --> 00:06:06,650
These hotkeys significantly speed up your work.

56
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:09,500
To add a new object to the scene, press Shift and A.

57
00:06:10,410 --> 00:06:12,120
The add object menu will open,

58
00:06:13,090 --> 00:06:14,770
where objects are grouped by categories.

59
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:18,180
For example, in the Mesh category,

60
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:23,640
there are cubes, spheres, cylinders, cones, and other geometric objects.

61
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:30,640
In this video, we covered the interface, navigation, and basic object operations: moving, rotating, and scaling.

62
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:34,420
In the next video, I will discuss more advanced Blender functions.

63
00:06:35,550 --> 00:06:37,430
Thank you for your attention!

64
00:06:37,430 --> 00:06:39,640
See you in the next videos!